I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the supplied analysis helpers first, then screen candidate phases and carry the defensible models through matched GSAS-II refinement branches. I’ll preserve stable project checkpoints and finish with the requested audits and cross-file consistency checks.

The chemical inventory strongly suggests a strontium–tungsten oxide reaction product, but I’m treating precursor carryover and intermediate oxides as live possibilities. I’m now extracting the experimental peak set and searching the supplied crystallographic library by both chemistry and diffraction evidence, so phase identity is not inferred from the nominal reaction alone.

Library screening identifies the main phase as scheelite SrWO₄ (I4₁/a): its strong reflections track the observed series from 18.32° through 95°. The coherent residual family at 23.45/23.97/24.73°, 34.08/34.49°, and higher angles is consistent with monoclinic WO₃. I rejected one superficially matching WO₃ entry because its atomistic model contains 1.57 Å O–O contacts; I’m carrying the clean P2₁/c model and the closest symmetry alternatives into matched refinements.

The first matched comparison already shows that scheelite alone leaves a substantial coherent residual (about 20% Rwp under the same deliberately minimal profile model). The clean monoclinic WO₃ branch improves the complete pattern and is refining with a positive scale; the remaining symmetry and precursor branches are still running under the identical nuisance model.

The matched inventory screen now favors two phases: scheelite SrWO₄ plus monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃. The clean monoclinic model outperforms triclinic and tetragonal WO₃ despite using fewer cell freedoms; SrCO₃ and Sr₃W₂O₉ collapse toward zero scale or unstable cells, and monoclinic SrWO₄ collapses to zero. I’m freezing this two-phase inventory and moving to the prescribed angular/profile branch tests.

The matched nuisance tests are decisive: polarization refines plausibly to 0.854 and gives the best stable envelope improvement; the two-parameter roughness model is rejected because its parameters are >99.9% correlated. A Gaussian angle term is also supported, while the standalone Lorentzian Y branch drives the width contribution negative and will not be retained. I’m now checking the joint Gaussian branch and total-width positivity before phase-specific size/strain tests.

Phase-specific size and strain do not earn retention: all four branches change Rwp by less than 0.0003%, and removing both phase contributions entirely is offset by the global profile with no fit loss. I’ve therefore neutralized those non-identifiable terms and will report no resolved crystallite-size or microstrain values. Next I’m screening simple one-parameter March–Dollase axes phase by phase.

Texture screening finds no supported scheelite orientation (ratios stay within ~2% of random and gains are negligible). The minor WO₃ phase shows a modest [110] March–Dollase signal (ratio ≈0.70, ΔRwp ≈0.052%), which I’m carrying provisionally. I’ll now inspect the regenerated residual and test coordinate/ADP changes before deciding whether that texture term survives.

The regenerated residual exposes an important inventory issue that the earlier minimal fits obscured: a very strong peak at 29.75° and a coherent family at 22.09°, 32.63°, 44.67°, 48.57°, 67.04°, and other angles are not explained by the two accepted structures. I’m reopening phase identification now, as required, and will not let profile or texture terms absorb that component.

The two-phase SrWO₄ + WO₃ model leaves a strong, coherent residual family, so I’m reopening the phase inventory before final refinement. I’ll audit and test the most chemically plausible minor phases from the same mature baseline, then index the residuals if none passes the full-pattern test.

The audits leave SrO, Sr₃W₂O₉, and monoclinic SrWO₄ structurally clean enough to test; the SrCO₃ model has half-occupied orientational disorder that needs caution, while tetragonal SrO₂’s short O–O contact is chemically consistent with peroxide. I’m now giving each candidate the same fixed mature baseline and refining only its positive scale and symmetry-allowed cell.

The fixed-cell candidates SrCO₃ and SrO₂ collapse toward zero/unsupported scale. SrO gives only a marginal, unstable cell response. Sr₃W₂O₉ is the first model with a substantial multi-peak gain, but its cell is still moving strongly; I’m letting that branch converge so I can check whether the final metric remains physically related to the source structure rather than becoming a free-cell overfit.

The refined Sr₃W₂O₉ metric is not a collapsed cell: it stays monoclinic C2/c and changes by about 3% in volume, while cutting full-pattern Rwp from 16.98% to 15.22%. Because a cell match alone is not identification, I’m now using that measured metric for a composition-blind library search to expose structural analogues and possible alternative chemistries before accepting it.

The composition-blind cell search ranks Sr₃W₂O₉ as the only chemically allowed close match among 16,232 monoclinic library entries; its source metric is the fourth-best overall. The current intensity residuals suggest orientation sensitivity, so I’m screening one-parameter March–Dollase axes before deciding whether the phase’s false high-angle predictions can be suppressed in a physically interpretable way.

The complete three-phase model remains stable after all scales, cells, and background were reconverged: Sr₃W₂O₉ stays positive at about 12.4 wt% before texture/profile retesting. The angular helper again selects sample displacement only (−574 µm); zero-only is worse, and the joint zero/shift model is rejected at 99.82% correlation. I’m now rebuilding the profile hierarchy from nominal instrument terms so inherited two-phase broadening and polarization cannot receive unearned credit.

The complete-profile retest supports a compact global model: W and U Gaussian terms, one positive Lorentzian X term, and polarization. The X+Y branch is slightly better but X/Y are 91.7% anticorrelated, so I’m retaining X alone for now. The reconverged model is at Rwp 13.97% with fractions near 77.7% SrWO₄, 10.0% WO₃, and 12.2% Sr₃W₂O₉; next I’m testing phase-specific size and strain independently.

The phase-size tests were negligible or unstable; only Sr₃W₂O₉ microstrain gave a broad, positive improvement across most angular regions. I’m carrying that term into a fresh stable baseline now, then I’ll test simple preferred-orientation models phase by phase and retain one only if its complete-pattern benefit survives reconvergence.

The first texture branches already show why the matched testing matters: SrWO₄ [001]/[100]/[110] offer only very small changes, while [112] is numerically erratic and is being rejected. I’m letting the monoclinic WO₃ and Sr₃W₂O₉ simple-axis branches finish before selecting anything.

Sr₃W₂O₉ is the only phase showing a clearly material texture signal. The [001] branch lowers the weighted residual substantially, but its parameter is converging slowly; I’m comparing it with [010], [100], [110], [101], and [011] so the axis choice is evidence-based rather than chosen from one strong line.

The Sr₃W₂O₉ [110] March–Dollase term is the clear simple-axis winner (ratio ≈1.67; regenerated Rwp 13.60% versus 13.92%). Its gain spans the 10–40°, 60–80° regions rather than a single overlap, though it worsens parts of 40–60° and 80–100°. I’m accepting it provisionally and now reconverging scales, background, and cells from that branch; the later matched-removal test must still confirm it.

The mature texture baseline changes the broadening balance somewhat, so I am repeating all six independent size/strain tests as required. Early results still reject SrWO₄ size (upper-bound behavior); SrWO₄ strain and WO₃ size/strain are being judged against positivity, stability, and multi-region benefit—not their tiny Rwp changes alone.

The mature broadening pass confirms only Sr₃W₂O₉ microstrain is defensible: it remains positive and materially improves the fit; SrWO₄ strain goes negative, SrWO₄ size hits its bound, and all other gains are negligible. With that model reconverged (Rwp ≈13.50%; crystalline fractions ≈76.1/9.7/14.3 wt%), I’m now testing limited coordinate refinements—oxygen in scheelite and separate heavy/oxygen subsets in the two monoclinic phases—while keeping ADPs and occupancies fixed.

Scheelite oxygen coordinates and WO₃ tungsten coordinates both give real intensity improvements with modest parameter counts. The 18-parameter WO₃ oxygen branch is much less stable (repeated damping/divergence), so even if its numerical fit is lower it will be rejected unless the covariance and exported geometry are unambiguously sound.

The geometry audit decisively rejects both oxygen-rich monoclinic coordinate branches: WO₃ develops 0.53–0.98 Å O–O contacts, and Sr₃W₂O₉ becomes non-parseable with ~99% parameter correlations. The limited branches remain chemically plausible: scheelite O coordinates are low-correlation and clean; WO₃ and Sr₃W₂O₉ heavy-atom branches retain six-coordinate W–O environments, though each introduces a short ~1.63–1.64 Å bond. I’m checking whether their gains are distributed across the pattern before carrying them forward sequentially.

Sequential testing resolves the overlap: once the scheelite oxygen coordinates are accepted, the WO₃ tungsten-coordinate branch no longer improves the pattern and is rejected. Sr₃W₂O₉ heavy-atom coordinates still lower Rwp by ~0.23 points across several regions, remain moderately correlated (~0.66), and pass the CIF geometry screen; the shortest W–O is 1.63 Å and the W bond-valence sum is somewhat high (~6.7), so I’ll retain the branch but explicitly qualify those coordinates as less certain than the phase identity.

After the two accepted coordinate updates, the model reconverges at Rwp ≈13.07% with provisional fractions 74.7% SrWO₄, 9.3% WO₃, and 16.0% Sr₃W₂O₉. I’m rerunning the prescribed angular competition now; the zero-only branch is already clearly inferior, while the displacement-only branch remains stable.

